The Hibernate upgrade from 5.4.31 to 7.4.5 is the highest-risk item in the iTicket persistence modernization and must be executed as a mandatory two-hop migration (5.x → 6.x → 7.x), as a direct 5→7 jump is unsupported. Beyond the mechanical javax.persistence → jakarta.persistence namespace change, the primary risk lies in silent behavioral changes that compile cleanly but fail at runtime. Key concerns include altered identifier-generation strategy (Hibernate 6 changes how AUTO/SEQUENCE maps to database sequences, risking ID collisions unless legacy behavior is pinned), the removal of the legacy Criteria API (session.createCriteria() with Restrictions must be rewritten to JPA CriteriaBuilder), changed multi-select query result types that surface as runtime ClassCastExceptions, and the removal of save()/update()/saveOrUpdate() in Hibernate 7, requiring refactoring to persist()/merge(). With roughly 56 HBM mapping files and over 1,000 session API call sites in iTicket, the change surface is extensive. The critical mitigation, given the application's very low test coverage, is to build data-layer integration tests before attempting the upgrade so silent regressions are caught pre-production. This track is tightly coupled to the Spring and Java 21 upgrades and should be sequenced and version-aligned with them, targeting a Jakarta Persistence 3.2 compatible baseline on the chosen runtime.
